Body:
I have a similar issue.

Model ID in my opencode config: "arn:aws:bedrock:eu-central-1:<accountId>:inference-profile/eu.anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-6". 
ModelID used by call to bedrock: "eu.arn:aws:bedrock:eu-central-1:<accountId>:inference-profile/eu.anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-6"

When I am using the model ID with aws CLI (aws bedrock-runtime converse --region eu-central-1 --model-id arn:aws: ...), it is working. So model id is correct.

 My opencode server configuration (SDK):

`
      openCodeServer = await createOpencode({
        port: config.OPENCODE_PORT,
        timeout: 30000,
        config: {
          logLevel: "DEBUG",
          model: "amazon-bedrock/anthropic-claude-sonnet-4-6",
          provider: {
            "amazon-bedrock": {
              options: {
                region: "eu-central-1"
              },
              models: {
                "anthropic-claude-sonnet-4-6": {
                  id: "arn:aws:bedrock:eu-central-1:xxxxxxxxx:inference-profile/eu.anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-6"
                }
              }
            }
          }
        }
      });
`

The error in response:
`
      "error": {
        "name": "APIError",
        "data": {
          "message": "undefined: The provided model identifier is invalid.",
          "statusCode": 400,
          "isRetryable": false,
          "responseHeaders": {
            "connection": "keep-alive",
            "content-length": "55",
            "content-type": "application/json",
            "date": "Thu, 07 May 2026 09:08:21 GMT",
            "x-amzn-errortype": "ValidationException:http://internal.amazon.com/coral/com.amazon.bedrock/",
            "x-amzn-requestid": "c3677a92-846e-49a1-8156-53a1776e3463"
          },
          "responseBody": "{\"message\":\"The provided model identifier is invalid.\"}",
          "metadata": {
            "url": "https://bedrock-runtime.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/model/eu.arn%3Aaws%3Abedrock%3Aeu-central-1%3Axxxxxxxxx3Ainference-profile%2Feu.anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-6/converse-stream"
          }
        }
      }
`

As a workaround, I'm now using legacy functions/function_call format as suggested in https://gist.github.com/daniel-farina/87dc1c394b94e45bb700d27e9ea03193 :

```
{"$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",
  "enabled_providers": ["llama.cpp"],
  "lsp": true,
  "permission": {"bash": "ask", "edit": "ask"},
  "provider": {
    "llama.cpp": {
      "npm": "@ai-sdk/openai-compatible", "name": "llama-server",
      "options": {"baseURL": "http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1", "toolParser": [{"type": "raw-function-call" }, {"type": "json"}]},
      "models": {
        "llama.cpp": {"name": "llama.cpp", "tool_call": true, "limit": {"context": 131072, "output": 131072}}
}}}}
```

This workaround only works when `-kvu` is not set.
